 "description": "The party colour palettes, by the palette a reader draws with: `elections` is the election domain's (packages/core/src/election-domain.mjs: partyColour, resolvePartyColour; a row marked registryWins is drawn in its colour here even where the result file has a colour of its own, the pre-1922 parties whose file colours are unreliable and three later ones); `mapLocal` and `mapRoi` are the map app's election palettes (packages/map/src/election/palette.mjs: the local and general one, and the Republic of Ireland's). `viewer` is the STV count viewer's table (site/app/election-viewer-package/js/stages2.js, a classic script the map app hands it to as globalThis.civgraphViewerColours): its party labels as the table wrote them, folded by the viewer's own normalisePartyKey (wave10-colours, 12 Nov 2026). `neutral` is the colour of a party with none of its own and `placeholders` the greys that, like it, mean no colour (election-domain.mjs NEUTRAL_PARTY_COLOUR, isPlaceholderPartyColour). Each is an ordered list keyed by the party name as normalizeName folds it; where a name is listed twice the later row's colour wins. Moved out of the code unchanged (12 Nov 2026, architecture rule R6); `note` keeps the reasons the code's comments gave. The pre-1922 rows (registryWins) follow Wikipedia's party colours (Module:Political party); where that colour is near-white or plain grey it would read as no data on the map, so a darker shade of the same hue (or the successor party's colour) is used, as the row's note says. The browser slice (packages/registries/src/browser/party-palettes.json) carries the colours alone.",
